<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ostrogoths</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ostrogoth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ostrogoths were the eastern branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, who lived north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and came under Hunnic rule in the late 4th century. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>' collapse they settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> as Roman federates, and in 488 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a> led them into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where they overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a> and ruled from Ravenna. Their kingdom was destroyed by Justinian's armies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothic-war-535-554">Gothic War</a>, which ended in 554.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ostrogoths.jpg" alt="Ostrogoths" width="320" height="533" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: commons:Eulenjäger; derivative work by Newsleep · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Theodoric_the_Great_Ravenna_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> appear in Roman records from the third century in the regions north of the Lower Danube and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. By then they already had sub-groups, among them the Tervingi, who bordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. The Ostrogoths are associated with the Greuthungi, who lived further east; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> put the dividing line between the two at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a>, with Alans beyond the Greuthungi near the Don.</p><p>Scholars disagree on that link. Herwig Wolfram sees Ostrogoths and Greuthungi as two names for one people, while Peter Heather argues that the Ostrogoths who followed Theodoric stand at the end of complex processes of fragmentation and unification, and some historians doubt that any single Gothic ethnicity united them before the Amal clan did. The name comes from a Germanic root meaning 'eastern', although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> took it simply to mean the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, as against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the late fourth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> subdued many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and Alans. Many Greuthungi entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in 376 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saphrax">Saphrax</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alatheus">Alatheus</a>, and a group of Ostrogoths and Greuthungi was settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> in the 380s. The Amal dynasty, which had fought under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>, settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> after the Hunnic empire collapsed, and around 483 or 484 the Amal kingdom took in the Thracian Goths as its second main component.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a>, born about 454 to Theodemir, was educated as a diplomatic hostage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Backed by the emperor Zeno, he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a> and ruled from Ravenna, keeping Roman administration and law and governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> under parallel systems. He was an Arian but allowed freedom of religion.</p><p>After his death in 526, his grandson Athalaric reigned under the regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalasuntha">Amalasuntha</a>, but Theodahad killed them both. In 535 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> began the Gothic War. Totila revived Gothic fortunes but was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-taginae">Battle of Taginae</a> in 552, and the kingdom had collapsed by 554. Survivors were absorbed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, who had their own kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> by 568.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Gothic language, best attested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulfilas">Ulfilas</a>'s translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, is the only eastern Germanic language with continuous surviving texts. The Edict of Theodoric of about 500 and the Variae of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassiodorus">Cassiodorus</a>, which can be read as the state papers of Theodoric and his successors, also survive. The historian Ian Wood stresses that Gothic identity in the West was a flexible, politicised construct formed through Roman political categories, military patronage and court ritual rather than a stable lineage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ostrogoths” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the <b>Ostrogoths</b>, at war with the Byzantine Empire, ceded their claims north of the Alps to the Merovingian kings around 536–537, all the Alemanni came under Frankish rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Clovis’s sons destroyed the Kingdom of the Thuringians in 531 and the Kingdom of the Burgundians in 534 and obtained Provence from the <b>Ostrogoths</b> in 537, and Frankish armies intervened repeatedly in Italy in the sixth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Venice grew out of the settlements that mainland Venetians founded on the islands of their lagoon from the fifth and sixth centuries, as Huns, <b>Ostrogoths</b> and above all the Lombards, who invaded Italy in 568, made the old Roman cities of the region unsafe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Gunthamund lost large parts of Sicily to Theodoric's <b>Ostrogoths</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bible was translated among the Visigothic Christians of Moesia, while the Codex Argenteus was preserved and passed on by northern <b>Ostrogoths</b> in present-day Italy.</blockquote><a href="/language/gothic">Gothic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Marching west into Belgica with Gepids, <b>Ostrogoths</b>, Alans and other vassals, Attila captured Metz, killing most of its people and burning the town; he also took Strasbourg.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-7">7 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
